{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Of David. Bless, my soul, YHWH</b> — it is the supernal soul." "<b>And all my innards</b> — the body is meant, which is the flesh." "And the word <i>bless</i> serves another function, and likewise it is: <b>Bless, my soul, YHWH, and bless, all my innards, His holy name</b>. Now he mentioned the proper noun [שם העצם], which is the glory, together with the soul, which is the glory — for the soul is the glory of the body, because the body counts for nothing except on account of the soul. Therefore he mentioned the proper noun explicitly together with the soul, and the Name together with the body, for thus it is written <i>and all my innards</i>, which are of the body, <i>His holy name</i>; and with the innards he did not mention the proper noun explicitly, only covertly."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 103, :notes {2 "שם העצם (literally 'the name of the substance/essence') rendered 'proper noun' throughout; Ibn Ezra appears to be drawing a grammatical distinction between the explicitly articulated divine name paired with נפשי and the more concealed reference paired with קרבי. The exact force of the grammatical terminology is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:37:57.632337957Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}